      "id": 1,
      "text": "Shenandoah was founded in 1837 by brothers Daniel and Henry Forrer who settled to exploit local natural resources.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shenandoah%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s railroad heritage is highlighted by the Big Gem Cast Iron Furnace, built alongside the Shenandoah Valley Railroad in the 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shenandoah%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Shenandoah sits between the Blue Ridge Mountains and Massanutten Ridge, just 8 miles west of Shenandoah National Park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shenandoah%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Big Gem Park, named after the historic furnace, covers 68.6 acres and offers outdoor recreation for residents.",
